    "result_text": "The entire test suite (all acceptance tests, the only tests in the repo) is green. Implementation complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `POST /api/owners` pipeline now supports the write-only `sharesHousehold` flag: when `true`, an owner is allowed to join an existing same-lastName/same-address household, and both owners receive a stable shared `householdId` that is returned on reads.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** \u2014 added a `householdId` field mapped to a new `household_id` column, with getter/setter.\n\n2. **Schema (`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`)** \u2014 added the `household_id` column to the `owners` table (tests run on hsqldb; `ddl-auto=none` so the SQL is authoritative).\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `householdId` property to the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it by name. (`sharesHousehold` already existed on `OwnerFields` as write-only.)\n\n4. **New `AssignHousehold` function** \u2014 runs after `BuildOwner` and before `SaveOwner`. When `sharesHousehold` is true it derives a deterministic 16-char identifier from the normalized `lastName|address` (SHA-256 prefix), assigns it to the new owner, and back-fills any existing same-household owner lacking one \u2014 so both sides share the same value regardless of creation order. Normalization (lowercase, collapsed whitespace) matches the existing `EnsureUniqueHousehold` guard.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `assignHousehold` step (governed by `transaction`) into the pipeline between `build` and `assignCustomerCode`.\n\nThe existing `EnsureUniqueHousehold` guard is unchanged \u2014 it still rejects duplicate households with 409 unless `sharesHousehold` opts in; the new step handles the shared-id assignment for the opt-in case.",
